7th and 18th centuries, particularly due to its strategic position along the Rhine River, which facilitated trade routes connecting Western Europe. The presence of influential families, such as the von der Heyden and the development of the linen industry, underscored its economic significance, making it a nexus of cultural and commercial exchange in the region.

